Section 51 of the Copyright Act, 1957 addresses when copyright in a work shall be deemed to be infringed. Based on the supplied evidence, infringement occurs in the following situations:

1. Under Section 51(a): When any person, without a licence granted by the owner of the copyright or the Registrar of Copyrights (or in contravention of the conditions of a licence or any condition imposed by a competent authority):
   - (i) does anything, the exclusive right to do which is by this Act conferred upon the owner of the copyright;
   - (ii) permits for profit any place to be used for the communication of the work to the public where such communication constitutes an infringement of the copyright in the work, unless he was not aware and had no reasonable ground for believing that such communication to the public would be an infringement of copyright.

2. Under Section 51(b): When any person:
   - (i) makes for sale or hire, or sells or lets for hire, or by way of trade displays or offers for sale or hire;
   - (ii) distributes either for the purpose of trade or to such an extent as to affect prejudicially the owner of the copyright;
   - (iii) by way of trade exhibits in public;
   - (iv) imports into India, any infringing copies of the work (with a proviso that nothing in sub-clause (iv) shall apply to the import of one copy of any work for the private and domestic use of the importer).

The section also includes an Explanation stating that, for the purposes of this section, the reproduction of a literary, dramatic, musical or artistic work in the form of a cinematograph film shall be deemed to be an "infringing copy".

Note: This is general legal information regarding the contents of Section 51 and does not constitute legal advice.

Under India's Biological Diversity Act, 2002, access and benefit sharing (ABS) is a regulatory framework aimed at ensuring fair and equitable sharing of benefits arising from the use of biological resources and associated knowledge. The National Biodiversity Authority (NBA) regulates access to biological resources by persons referred to in sections 3, 4 and 6 of the Act, and issues guidelines governing such access and the fair and equitable sharing of benefits.

When granting approvals for obtaining or transferring biological resources or associated knowledge (under sections 19 and 20), the NBA imposes terms and conditions designed to secure equitable sharing of benefits derived from the accessed biological resources, their by-products, innovations, associated practices, and related knowledge. These benefits are to be determined in accordance with mutually agreed terms between the applicant, the concerned local bodies, and the 'benefit claimers'—defined as the conservers of biological resources, their by-products, and the creators and holders of associated knowledge, innovations, and practices.

The NBA determines the manner of benefit sharing, which may take any of the following forms:
(a) grant of joint ownership of intellectual property rights to the NBA, or to identified benefit claimers;
(b) transfer of technology;
(c) location of production, research and development units in areas that will improve the living standards of benefit claimers;
(d) association of Indian scientists, benefit claimers, and local people with research and development, bio-survey, and bio-utilization activities;
(e) setting up of a venture capital fund to aid benefit claimers; and
(f) payment of monetary compensation and non-monetary benefits to benefit claimers as the NBA deems fit.

Where monetary benefit sharing is ordered, the NBA may direct the amount to be deposited in the National Biodiversity Fund. Where the biological resource or knowledge was accessed from specific individuals, groups, or organizations, the NBA may direct that the amount be paid directly to them in accordance with any applicable agreement. The NBA is also empowered, in consultation with the Central Government, to frame regulations/guidelines on benefit sharing, and may impose charges by way of royalty as a condition of approval.
